### What is this question really testing?

The interviewer is checking whether you can organize ambiguity into a plan and drive it to completion. They want to see scope definition, milestone breakdown, risk management, and a quantified result.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class LeadershipTest {
    enum Signal { LEADER, CONTRIBUTOR }
    public static Signal evaluate(boolean definedScope, boolean organizedWork, boolean deliveredResult) {
        return (definedScope && organizedWork && deliveredResult) ? Signal.LEADER : Signal.CONTRIBUTOR;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(evaluate(true, true, true)); // LEADER
    }
}
```

### How do you define scope and success metrics?

Set a clear, measurable goal at the start. "Transfers settle in under five seconds end to end" is specific. "Make transfers faster" is not.

```java
package com.bank.service;
public class ScopeDefinition {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String goal = "Transfers settle in under 5 seconds end to end";
        String inScope = "Transfer validation, real-time ledger, fraud check, notification";
        String outOfScope = "Historical statement redesign, mobile app changes";
        System.out.println("Goal: " + goal);
        System.out.println("In scope: " + inScope);
        System.out.println("Out of scope: " + outOfScope);
    }
}
```

### How do you break work into milestones with owners?

Each milestone has a named owner and an explicit dependency, so everyone sees not just their own piece but how it fits the critical path.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.List;
public class MilestonePlanner {
    record Milestone(String name, String owner, String dependency, String status) {}
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Milestone> plan = List.of(
            new Milestone("Transfer validation API", "Amit",   "None",               "Done"),
            new Milestone("Real-time ledger update",  "Me",     "Transfer validation", "In progress"),
            new Milestone("Fraud check integration",  "Neha",   "Ledger update",       "Blocked"),
            new Milestone("Customer notification",    "Rajesh", "Fraud check",         "Not started")
        );
        plan.forEach(m -> System.out.println(m.name() + " [" + m.owner() + "] dep=" + m.dependency() + " status=" + m.status()));
    }
}
```

```text
Transfer validation API [Amit] dep=None status=Done
Real-time ledger update [Me] dep=Transfer validation status=In progress
Fraud check integration [Neha] dep=Ledger update status=Blocked
Customer notification [Rajesh] dep=Fraud check status=Not started
```

### How do you unblock dependencies?

When a milestone is blocked, you do not just wait. You work with the blocking team to resolve the dependency and keep the critical path moving.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class Unblocking {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Blocked: Fraud check waiting on risk team API contract");
        System.out.println("Action: I met with risk team, aligned on contract, unblocked Neha");
        System.out.println("Result: fraud milestone resumed same day");
    }
}
```

### How do you protect scope?

Push back on nice-to-have additions that would endanger the launch date. "We can add multi-currency in v2. v1 ships on time with core transfers."

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class ScopeProtection {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Request: 'Add multi-currency support to v1'");
        System.out.println("Response: 'Multi-currency is valuable but adds 3 weeks risk.");
        System.out.println("  v1 ships on time with INR transfers.");
        System.out.println("  Multi-currency goes into v2 immediately after launch.'");
    }
}
```

### How do you sequence risk?

Tackle the riskiest unknowns first, not last. If the fraud-check integration has the most uncertainty, do it early so surprises surface when you still have time.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class RiskSequencing {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("High risk: fraud-check integration (external dependency) -> tackle first");
        System.out.println("Medium risk: ledger update (internal, well-understood) -> middle");
        System.out.println("Low risk: notification (simple, no dependency) -> last");
    }
}
```

### What is the leader's value versus doing the work yourself?

Your job is direction, unblocking, and decision-making. You set the scope, remove obstacles, and make calls when the team is stuck.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class LeaderValue {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Not my job: code every milestone myself");
        System.out.println("My job: define scope, assign owners, track critical path");
        System.out.println("My job: unblock dependencies, protect scope, make decisions");
        System.out.println("My job: deliver a measurable result on time");
    }
}
```

### What was the quantified result?

Transfers that used to take hours now complete in seconds. The project launched on schedule with settlement comfortably under the five-second target.

```java
package com.bank.service;
import java.util.logging.Logger;
public class ProjectResult {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger(ProjectResult.class.getName());
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        log.info("Before: transfers settled in batch cycle (hours)");
        log.info("After: transfers settle in under 5 seconds");
        log.info("Launched: on schedule, all milestones delivered");
    }
}
```
